# The remote squad_lead leaf node: the two servers it needs, and the one event
# row both paths carry. bin/jg-bus-leaf.sh reads it, a home's config/bus-leaf
# overrides any single key here, and JG_BUS_LEAF_<KEY> overrides both.
# docs/bus-leaf-shadow.md owns what this step proves and what it does not.
#
# The leaf is in SHADOW: this file starts nothing by itself, the SSH route keeps
# carrying every real delivery, and `jg-bus-leaf.sh parity` is what compares the
# two paths.
#   subject          the one subject the leaf carries. Interest decides what
#                    crosses the leaf connection, so an event nobody subscribes to
#                    crosses nothing.
#   leaf-name        this remote home's server name, so two leaves on one hub are
#                    told apart in its logs.
#   leaf-user/-pass  the identity a leaf must present. The shipped value is the
#                    local-only placeholder; a home that leaves 127.0.0.1 sets a
#                    real one in config/bus-leaf, next to its other private state.
#   hub-leaf-listen  the home bus side's leaf listener, rendered into its config.
#   hub-leaf-dial    what the remote home dials. It differs from the listener when
#                    the bus listens on a loopback or wildcard address and the
#                    remote home reaches it by another name.
#   leaf-listen      the remote home's own server, which is where its lead's
#                    clients read.
#   url              the client address this side talks to as a client: the home
#                    bus on the main home, and the leaf's own address in the remote
#                    home's config/bus-leaf, because a client there reads its own
#                    server.
set subject jg.leaf
set leaf-name remote-lead
set leaf-user juggernaut-leaf
set leaf-pass juggernaut-leaf
set hub-leaf-listen 127.0.0.1:7422
set hub-leaf-dial 127.0.0.1:7422
set leaf-listen 127.0.0.1:4333
set url nats://127.0.0.1:4222
